      "editorial": {
        "en": {
          "why_it_matters": "The appeal is the collision of three highly specific instrumental languages with the JLCO rather than a generic world-jazz theme. Shamma brings the oud and an Arab classical vocabulary, de Holanda has stretched the Brazilian bandolim into a virtuosic improvising voice, and Domínguez has spent decades making flamenco rhythm and harmony converse with jazz piano. Putting all three inside Marsalis's large-ensemble language, during his final JALC season, creates a serious test of whether the orchestra can make those traditions speak to one another without sanding off their differences.",
          "trip_verdict": "Actively make room for one of the two nights if a New York trip overlaps, especially if instrumental identity and cross-cultural improvisation are central to the listening thread. Two performances provide some flexibility, so this is a strong regional-trip anchor rather than a date that should automatically override every other plan.",
